---
/**
 * Decision Tool template — working client-side estimator with on-page
 * methodology, assumptions, limitations, and explicit confidence language.
 */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SampleNotice from '../../components/SampleNotice.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import TrustMeta from '../../components/TrustMeta.astro';

const related = [
  {
    title: 'Commercial Roof Life Expectancy Benchmarks',
    href: '/data-research/commercial-roof-life-expectancy-benchmarks/',
    type: 'Dataset',
    description: 'The benchmark data behind this estimator.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Repair or Replace? How to Decide',
    href: '/knowledge/guides/repair-or-replace/',
    type: 'Decision Guide',
    description: 'What to do with the estimate once you have it.',
  },
  {
    title: 'CRI Methodology',
    href: '/about/methodology/',
    type: 'Governance',
    description: 'How every CRI tool is built and validated.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Roof Life Expectancy Estimator"
  description="CRI's Roof Life Expectancy Estimator is in development and does not yet produce estimates. Its methodology is published here for scrutiny while the underlying benchmark dataset is validated."
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ name: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' }, { name: 'Roof Life Expectancy Estimator' }]} />

  {/* Deliberately INDEXED: the page's entire content explains its own gating, so it can
    rank for its name without misleading anyone. The tool itself is gated (no form, no
    output) pending Benchmarks v1.0 — see the GATED comment below. */}
  <article class="container section--tight">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow" data-pagefind-filter="type">Decision Tool</p>
      <h1>Roof Life Expectancy Estimator</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        In development. This tool does not yet produce estimates — its benchmark dataset is
        still preliminary. The methodology is published below so it can be examined before
        anyone is asked to trust it.
      </p>
      {/* No sources= : this tool lists no external citations, and its own methodology
          states the modifier values are pre-validation placeholders. It previously
          hardcoded sources={12} while citing nothing. */}
      <TrustMeta updated="2026-07-16" status="preliminary" />
    </header>

    <SampleNotice status="preliminary" />

    {/* GATED — the interactive estimator is deliberately removed, not hidden.
        Its modifiers are unvalidated v0.1 placeholders drawn from a dataset that is
        explicitly "not for citation". A tool that emits confident year-ranges from
        placeholder data, on a contractor-affiliated site, is the worst-case version of
        this project. The methodology below stays published so it can be examined and
        challenged before the numbers are trusted. Restore the form, the result panel,
        and the calculation script when Benchmarks v1.0 is validated. */}
    <div class="callout callout--caution" style="margin-top: var(--space-5);">
      <strong class="callout__title">In development — not yet validated</strong>
      <p>
        The Roof Life Expectancy Estimator is not currently available, and it will not
        produce an estimate. Its underlying dataset — the
        <a href="/data-research/commercial-roof-life-expectancy-benchmarks/">CRI Roof Life
        Expectancy Benchmarks</a> — is still preliminary, and the modifier values below are
        pre-validation placeholders. A tool that returns a confident number from data we
        have not validated would be worse than no tool at all, so it returns nothing.
      </p>
      <p>
        We publish the methodology anyway, in full, below. That is deliberate: you should
        be able to examine and challenge how a tool works <em>before</em> deciding whether
        to trust its output — not after. It is here to be audited, not used.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What to use instead:</strong> the remaining service life of a specific roof
        is established by physical assessment — a visual survey, a moisture scan, and core
        samples where warranted — not by a population-level model. If you are facing the
        decision this tool was meant to inform, work through
        <a href="/knowledge/guides/repair-or-replace/">Repair or Replace? How to Decide</a>,
        which is published and sourced.
      </p>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
        This page will be updated when Benchmarks v1.0 completes validation. We would rather
        ship it late than ship it wrong.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top: var(--space-7);">
      <h2 id="methodology">Methodology (published for scrutiny — not in operation)</h2>
      <p>
        This is the method the estimator <em>will</em> use once its inputs are validated. No
        part of it is running today. The modifier values below are placeholders: they are
        plausible, they are not yet evidence.
      </p>
      <p>
        The estimator starts from the service-life ranges in the
        <a href="/data-research/commercial-roof-life-expectancy-benchmarks/">CRI Roof Life
        Expectancy Benchmarks</a> (v0.1), then applies documented modifiers:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Climate:</strong> hot/high-UV −10%, freeze-thaw −8%, coastal/hurricane −15%, moderate ±0.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> proactive +12%, reactive ±0, minimal/unknown −18%.</li>
        <li><strong>Ponding water:</strong> −12% where reported. “Not sure” applies no modifier but lowers the stated confidence, because ponding is a major unobserved variable.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Remaining life = adjusted range minus current age, floored at zero. Outputs are
        ranges by design; point estimates would overstate precision. The confidence label
        reflects how much of the variance the tool cannot see: it drops when inputs are
        unknown, when the roof is near or past the benchmark range, and always remains at
        most “moderate” until benchmarks v1.0 validation is complete.
      </p>

      <h2>Limitations</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Does not model installation quality — the single largest variance driver in field studies.</li>
        <li>Does not detect existing moisture intrusion; only a survey can.</li>
        <li>Assumes the system was appropriate for the building at installation.</li>
        <li>Benchmark modifiers are v0.1 placeholders pending validation against field data.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </article>

  <RelatedLinks items={related} />

</Base>
